deep.reconstruction:
This page contains no mathematics. It is a fair copy of excerpts, in English, from John Locke, An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, Book II, Chapter XXI ('Of Power'). The text matches the later editions (2nd edition 1694 onward), where Locke revised the chapter on liberty and added §71, 'Recapitulation'.

(1) The page opens with Locke's marginal section headings for §§41-46. The quoted heading 'The greatest good, not being desired, moves not the will' is the summary of §45. 'Due consideration raises desire' heads §46.

(2) A quotation from §46 follows: 'by a due consideration, and examining any good proposed, it is in our power to raise our desires in a due proportion to the value of that good...'

(3) Next comes the heading of §47, 'The power to suspend the prosecution of any desire makes way for consideration', and an extended quotation from §47. Its core claims are:
- the most pressing uneasiness usually, but not always, determines the will;
- the mind can suspend the execution of its desires and weigh their objects;
- 'In this lies the liberty man has';
- errors come from precipitating the determination of the will;
- this power of suspension is 'the source of all liberty', and in it consists what is improperly called free will;
- it is a perfection of our nature to desire, will and act according to the last result of a fair examination.

(4) Finally there is a long quotation from §71, 'Recapitulation'. Here Locke rejects locating liberty in an 'indifferency of the man' that precedes the judgment of the understanding. He allows 'indifferency' only in the sense of the operative powers, which remain able to act or forbear after, as before, the decree of the will: 'as far as this indifferency reaches, a man is free, and no farther.'

Context: the previous page is German prose on causality and the regress of causes, in the Herbart-style metaphysics found in Riemann's Naturphilosophie papers. The next page is evidently a second, rougher copy of the same Locke passage. The folio thus documents reading notes on Locke's treatment of freedom of the will, presumably in connection with Riemann's interest in Herbartian psychology and the will. Whether the hand is Riemann's own is not established by this page alone. The neat English copperplate-like script could be his or a copyist's.

There are no derivations, computations, or mathematical claims to reconstruct.
